Files of upstream Ghidra that Hydra Engine modified or replaced
(every file below exists in upstream Ghidra; compared with the upstream commit named in
CHANGELOG.md; the list was re-checked for 12.3-hydra.4 with `git diff --name-status` against
upstream commit 1b9e58a915cf867e106495eaa2a4b18eb4971c9d, outside the HeadlessSession module,
.github and the changelog: it shows these modified files and the added ones named further below):

  Ghidra/Features/Base/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/core/osgi/OSGiParallelLock.java
      The threads of one JVM take an in-JVM lock keyed by the lock file before the file lock, so a
      second script load in the same process waits instead of failing with
      OverlappingFileLockException; other processes are excluded by the file lock as before.
  Ghidra/Framework/DB/src/main/java/db/Database.java
      When the folder of a database cannot be listed right after a save gave the new version its
      final name (on Windows, another process holds the folder), the new version is recorded
      without the listing instead of failing a save that has already taken effect on disk. A
      comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/DB/src/main/java/db/buffers/LocalBufferFile.java
      A temporary buffer file is truncated to zero length before it is closed and deleted, so
      that ReFS (as on a Windows Dev Drive) does not write its unwritten pages back first.
      A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/DB/src/main/java/db/buffers/LocalManagedBufferFile.java
      The header of a new version file is written and synced before the file is renamed to its
      final name, so a process killed during a save no longer leaves a final-named version file
      with an empty header. A comment at the top of the file says so.
      After a save that failed without leaving a new version, a new pre-save is started, so that
      the open program can be saved again once the cause (a file held by another process) is gone.
  Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/db/VersionedDatabase.java
      The newest version file is checked when a database is opened (see VersionFileCheck below).
      A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/local/LocalDatabaseItem.java
      A new private database item gets its file type only once its content is complete, so an
      item whose writer was killed is removed as incomplete instead of being listed as a program
      that cannot be opened. A comment at the top of the file says so.
      The newest version is also checked when an item is opened; an unreadable newest version of
      a disposable, writable item is quarantined and the previous version is used, and an item
      opened read-only is never changed (answers corrupt_newest).
      An item whose folder holds a newer version than the one it recorded scans its folder again
      when it is opened, and an item whose folder cannot be listed for a moment is kept by a
      refresh instead of being dropped from the project.
  Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/local/LocalFolderItem.java
      An item whose property file cannot be read for a moment (another process holds it without
      read sharing) is kept by a refresh with the properties it read before, instead of being
      dropped from the project; a property file that can be read and does not parse is still an
      error. A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/Generic/src/main/java/generic/util/FileChannelLock.java
      Probes of one lock file from two threads of one JVM take turns (JVM_TURN), so a probe never
      reads "held" for a lock nobody holds, nor makes a concurrent writable open fail.
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/SleighLanguageFile.java
      The language lock file is created the first time a language needs it instead of for every
      language description at start-up. A comment at the top of the file says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/SleighLanguage.java
      The .sla file is decoded with a TemplateInterner open, so equal p-code templates of the
      language share one object. A comment at that line says so.
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/template/ConstTpl.java
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/template/VarnodeTpl.java
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/template/HandleTpl.java
  Ghidra/Framework/SoftwareModeling/src/main/java/ghidra/app/plugin/processors/sleigh/template/OpTpl.java
      ConstTpl and VarnodeTpl gain decodeShared, which returns the shared template of an equal
      value while a TemplateInterner (a new Hydra file in the same folder) is open; HandleTpl,
      VarnodeTpl and OpTpl decode their parts through it. Without a table open they decode as
      before.
  gradle/distributableGhidraModule.gradle
      Two lines: a module's support/ folder is copied into the distribution without
      __pycache__ folders and .pyc files.
  build.gradle
      One line at the end: `apply from: 'gradle/hydra/headless.gradle'`.
  .gitattributes
      Three lines: scripts in .github/scripts keep LF line endings.
  README.md
      Replaced by Hydra Engine's README. Ghidra's original README is kept, byte for byte, as
      README-GHIDRA.md (a new file).
  .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md, feature_request.md, question.md
      Removed; replaced by issue forms (bug_report.yml, feature_request.yml, config.yml).

Every other file of upstream Ghidra, including its .github/workflows, is as published by its
authors.


What Hydra Engine adds to Ghidra (new files, none of them in upstream Ghidra):
  - the HeadlessSession module (Ghidra/Features/HeadlessSession): a shared headless session
    daemon, its Python launcher and client, and their tests;
  - a headless-only build (gradle/hydra/headless.gradle, the buildHeadless task);
  - Ghidra/Framework/FileSystem/src/main/java/ghidra/framework/store/db/VersionFileCheck.java, one
    added class in a Ghidra package (header validation and the quarantine of an unreadable newest
    version file);
  - the repository's own documentation, assets and CI (NOTICE-HYDRA, CHANGELOG.md,
    README-GHIDRA.md, and the files in .github/: CONTRIBUTING.md, SECURITY.md, issue forms, the
    pull request template, assets, scripts, and the ci.yml, release.yml and upstream-sync.yml
    workflows), and the release notes in docs/.


